f='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "4636"} "1912"] "\n" [:option {:value "4715"} "1920"] "\n" [:option {:value "4795"} "1924"] "\n" [:option {:value "4893"} "1928"] "\n" [:option {:value "4989"} "1932"] "\n" [:option {:value "5087"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "5197"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "5325"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "5441"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "5588"} "1960"] "\n" [:option {:value "5770"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "6025"} "1968"] "\n" [:option {:value "6335"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "6612"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "6863"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "7131"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "7468"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "7825"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "8171"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "8567"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "8956"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "261692"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "357188"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "19000181"} "2020"] "\n" [:option {:value "2001688"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/588"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "100 metres Freestyle, Women" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "1 –  2 August 2012"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/855"} "London Aquatics Centre, Olympic Park, Stratford, London"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/322262"} "48 from 43 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "After her 2008 Olympic title, " [:a {:href "/athletes/93488"} "Britta Steffen"] " also won the 2009 world title. With the swimsuit craze at its zenith, she lowered the world record in Rome to a spectacular 52.07. With the new swimsuits banned from 2010, times dropped back into the 53-second range. At the 2011 World Championships, the title was shared between two outsiders, " [:a {:href "/athletes/116762"} "Aleksandra Gerasimenya"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/103775"} "Jeanette Ottesen"] ". Third then was " [:a {:href "/athletes/112317"} "Ranomi Kromowidjojo"] ", who would become the first to swim below 53 seconds in the post-polyurethane era, when she clocked 52.75 in spring 2012. At the 4×100 m relay earlier in the Games, Kromowidjojo had by far clocked the fasted relay split, despite losing out to Australia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/112322"} "Mel Schlanger"] " in the final leg. Other favorites for the gold medal included Sweden’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/112272"} "Sarah Sjöström"] ", 2012 European Champion and swimming 53.05 in late 2011, and home favorite " [:a {:href "/athletes/112268"} "Fran Halsall"] ". Veteran swimmer " [:a {:href "/athletes/50214"} "Therese Alshammar"] " suffered from a trapped nerve shortly before the Games, and decided to skip the event."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The semi-finals saw Steffen’s Olympic Record fall, Kromowidjojo lowering it to 53.05. Steffen herself was eliminated, as was Sjöström. Second best was Schlanger, three tenths back. In the final, it was Gerasimenya off to the best start, swimming from lane 1 (she had won the world title from lane 8). Kromowidjojo turned in fourth place, but had the best second leg and powered to 53.00, again lowering the Olympic record. Second was Gerasimenya, winning Belarus’ first Olympic medal in swimming, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/112313"} "Tang Yi"] " claming a surprise bronze."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Born of a Surinamese father and a Dutch mother, Kromowidjojo won her first Olympic title at age 17 in Beijing with the Dutch relay team. The squad repeated at the 2009 and 2011 Worlds, but Kromowidjojo was yet to win an individual title in the 50 m pool. She did win two golds at the 2010 World Short Course Championships in Dubai. Earlier that year, she had been diagnosed with meningitis, causing her to miss the 2010 Europeans."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/112317"} "Ranomi Kromowidjojo"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]] [:td {} "53.66 (3 h7)"] [:td {} "53.05 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "53.00 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/116762"} "Aliaksandra Herasimenia"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BLR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BLR.png"}] "BLR"]] [:td {} "53.63 (2 h7)"] [:td {} "53.78 (2 h1)"] [:td {} "53.38 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/112313"} "Tang Yi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CHN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CHN.png"}] "CHN"]] [:td {} "53.28 (1 h5)"] [:td {} "53.60 (3 h2)"] [:td {} "53.44 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/112322"} "Mel Schlanger"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUS.png"}] "AUS"]] [:td {} "53.50 (1 h7)"] [:td {} "53.38 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "53.47 (4)"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "5"] [:td {} [:a {:hr